    "q-118440": {
      "dom_id": "step-q118440",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256222/assets/b5a893624b5dd480d0be467c4e0947447e5527c4477e115402e5b08f89da17f8.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could be used to describe the graph shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256222/assets/d52b7e3ce47e0c710fc120337af1fd6a6435ad2a555289877e8752bd6bfbf873.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis.\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x).$\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)=-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ However, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is not among the given options.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
